course: technology
lesson: 37
title: The internet and undersea cables
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The internet is not one machine; it is a network of networks, millions of computers joined together. When you open a web page, it is chopped into small packets, like a big jigsaw posted in many envelopes, and each packet hops through machines called routers, maybe along different roads, until they are put back together on your screen.
explain: To cross oceans, most of the internet does not fly through space. It goes through cables lying on the sea floor, with glass threads as thin as a hair inside, carrying flashes of light from one continent to another.
explain: At the far end are data centres, huge buildings full of computers called servers, humming and kept cool, that store websites, videos and emails and send them out when you ask.
explain: The internet also has tricksters. A message that pretends to be your bank, a game or a friend and asks for a password or money is a scam: never give passwords, never click strange links, and always tell an adult.
fact: The internet is a network of networks: data is sent in small packets that hop through routers along many paths and are put back together at the end, and if a packet gets lost it is simply sent again; a single web page can come in hundreds of packets.
fact: Don't share personal information online, and tell an adult if something online upsets you.
fact: Most data between continents travels through undersea cables, not satellites; inside, thin glass fibres carry pulses of light; there are many such cables, so if a ship's anchor cuts one, data goes round by the others, maybe more slowly, until it is mended.
fact: In a fibre-optic cable, light bounces along a hair-thin glass thread, carrying data as flashes, and it can travel a long way before it needs boosting; never look into the end of a fibre.
fact: A website is a set of files stored on a server and sent to your browser, which shows them as a page; the World Wide Web was invented by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989.
fact: A search engine uses programs that crawl the web and build a huge index, then ranks pages for your words; results are not always true, so check sources.
fact: A data centre is a building full of computers called servers, with cooling and backup power, and some are built in cold places to save on cooling.
fact: When you stream a film it is sent in small pieces as you watch, with a little stored ahead, and the quality changes with the speed of your connection; when the stored part runs out, it buffers.
fact: An email is sent to your email provider's server and passed to the other person's server; the @ sign was chosen for email in 1971.
fact: Online scams are fake messages that pretend to be someone you trust to get passwords or money; banks will never ask for your password by message.
fact: Never give your password to anyone online, never click strange links or open attachments from strangers, and tell an adult.
fact: A smart speaker listens for a wake word, and only then sends your request to computers online for an answer; ask an adult before using one, because it may store recordings.
exercise: What is the internet? | A network of networks joining millions of computers | One giant computer in America | A satellite that sends web pages | A cable from your house to the shop
exercise: How does data travel across the internet? | In small packets that hop through routers | As one long piece along a single wire from start to end | As sound waves through the air | On discs carried by post vans
exercise: How does most internet data cross the ocean? | Through cables on the sea floor | By satellites only | By radio waves bouncing off the sea | On ships carrying hard drives
exercise: What carries the data inside a fibre-optic cable? | Flashes of light along thin glass threads | Water flowing very fast through a narrow pipe under the sea | Sound echoing down a tube | Tiny electric sparks jumping
exercise: Who invented the World Wide Web, and when? | Tim Berners-Lee, in 1989 | Alessandro Volta, in 1800 | Ada Lovelace, in 1843 | Percy Shaw, in 1934
exercise: What is a website, really? | Files on a server that your browser shows as a page | A picture painted inside your screen | A program that lives only on your phone and nowhere else at all | A cable that joins two houses
exercise: How does a search engine find answers so fast? | It has already built a huge index of pages | It reads the whole web, every single page, after you type your question | It asks a person to look things up | It guesses the answer from your words
exercise: Are search results always true? | No, so you should check sources | Yes, the top result is always true | Yes, every page is checked by the police | Only the results with pictures are
exercise: What is inside a data centre? | Many computers called servers, with cooling | Big tanks of internet water | Thousands of people typing web pages by hand all day and night | Satellites waiting to be launched
exercise: Why are some data centres built in cold countries? | It saves on cooling the computers | Computers only work below freezing | Cold air makes the internet faster | The servers like the snow
exercise: Why does a film sometimes buffer when you stream it? | The pieces stored ahead have run out | The film is being drawn by hand as you watch it | The TV is too old to show new films properly | The film's actors are on a break from filming
exercise: Which sign was chosen for email addresses in 1971? | @ | the hash sign | & | %
exercise: What is an online scam? | A fake message pretending to be someone you trust | A real message from your bank about your savings account | A game that is free to play and has no adverts at all | A slow internet connection that keeps dropping out
exercise: Will your bank ever ask for your password by message? | No, never | Yes, once a year | Yes, if it is urgent | Only by email
exercise: When does a smart speaker start listening fully? | After it hears its wake word | All day and all night, recording everything | Only when the TV is on | Only when someone sings
exercise: What should you do with a link from a stranger? | Not click it, and tell an adult | Click it to see what it is about | Send it to all your friends to check | Click it twice to be sure it works
test: «got a msg: YOU WON A NEW CONSOLE, just type your password here» What should you do? | Not reply or type anything, and tell an adult | Type the password quickly before the offer runs out and someone else gets it | Send your address instead of a password | Reply asking which colour console
test: «my cousin says the internet comes from space, satellites do it all» What is the best correction? | Most data between continents goes through undersea cables | Right, satellites carry all of it, every message and every film you watch | It comes from the Moon | It travels on the wind
test: «first result says sharks are mammals so it's true lol» What is the best answer? | Search results can be wrong; check good sources | The first result is always true | If it is on the internet it is true, because someone checked it before it went up | Only the last result can be wrong
test: What would happen if a ship's anchor cut an undersea cable? | Data would be sent along other cables, maybe more slowly, until it is repaired | The whole internet would stop forever | Nothing could ever cross that ocean again | The sea would fill with data
test: In a cartoon, a whole web page zooms down one wire as one big parcel. What really happens? | It is split into many small packets that may take different paths | It is sent as a single picture | It is read out loud to your computer | It is printed and posted
test: Count them: a web page comes in 300 packets and 100 have arrived. How many are still on the way? | 200 | 100 | 300 | 400
test: Count them: you send 3 emails to each of 4 friends. How many emails? | 12 | 7 | 3 | 16
test: Count them: a film has 90 minutes and the app stores 2 minutes ahead. You have watched 30 minutes. How far into the film has the app loaded? | 32 minutes | 30 minutes | 60 minutes | 92 minutes
test: «is that speaker thing always spying on us, creepy» What is closest to the truth? | It listens fully only after its wake word, but it may store recordings | It records every word all day | It cannot hear anything at all | It only listens to the dog
test: «why do they put computers in Iceland, no one lives there lol» What is a real reason? | Cold air helps keep the servers cool | Servers only work properly on ice | Iceland has no internet laws at all | Data travels much faster in the snow
test: Why is a web page chopped into packets instead of sent all in one piece? | Small packets can each find a free path and a lost one can be sent again | Packets are prettier on the screen | Big pieces are not allowed by law | Packets make the page smaller on your screen
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the internet? | A network of networks through which data is transmitted in packets via routers | A single computer of great size situated in one country | A satellite which broadcasts web pages to all dwellings | A cable connecting each dwelling to a single shop
exam: Identify the principal means by which data is conveyed between continents. | Cables laid upon the sea floor containing glass fibres | Satellites exclusively | Radio waves reflected from the surface of the ocean between the continents | Vessels transporting storage devices
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning requests for a password received by message. | A bank shall never request a password by message, and such a message is to be reported to an adult | A bank shall request a password by message annually | A password may be disclosed where the matter is urgent | A password may be disclosed where a prize is offered
exam: Ascertain the reason for which certain data centres are constructed in cold regions. | The expense of cooling the servers is thereby reduced | Computers function solely below freezing | Cold air increases the velocity of data | Snow improves the reliability of the electricity supplied to such establishments
